{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"POST-3d57 ASSESSMENT (2026-08-22, /rebar-debug Phase 1 discipline, worktree at 8481a057b69 = origin/main, includes 14aa285f963 / change 2123). Question: does 3d57's pin-health fix resolve the gate-check overruns? ANSWER: NO — the mechanism is independent and fully persists post-fix. Verdict: architectural (operator decision needed), per this ticket's own \"Decision needed\" framing.\n\nMEASUREMENTS ON THE LIVE STORE (5,269 tickets, multi-session load, post-14aa285f963):\n- `rebar claim` on this ticket: gate warning \"took 2296.7 ms (> 20 ms budget)\"; command wall 53.5s (most of the rest was tickets-branch push contention/retry).\n- Instrumented `queue.pending_enrichment` probes: cold/full scan 8097.7 ms; marker-scoped probes 2840.8 / 2870.8 / 3249.7 ms. Same order as the pre-fix 339-3589 ms observations — no improvement.\n- `rebar enrich status`: {\"pending\": 1046, \"claimed\": 0, \"soaking\": 1} — a ~1,050-entry standing backlog (~20% of the store).\n- Drain lock free at probe time, one detached drain child running (pid 52083, spawned from a retired impl worktree's venv, 0:24 CPU).\n\nWHY 3d57 DID NOT FIX IT: change 2123 touched only pin health (relation_snapshot child-index snapshot); zero commits to llm/enrich_drain.py or llm/overlap/queue.py between 14aa285f963 and HEAD. 3d57's own RCA rejected the enrich gate as the show-stall driver (rival (c)) and recorded it as a separate write-path mechanism — confirmed here empirically.\n\nMECHANISM, REFINED (beyond the marker-clear story in the description): the task-4144 live-set fast path is ENGAGED and still costs ~3s, because the scoped walk is O(backlog) — it re-reduces all ~1,049 live entries per probe. So the overrun no longer needs marker churn at all: the standing backlog alone prices every probe at seconds. The backlog stands because drain throughput is structurally below the enqueue rate: batch cap 5/run (overlap_drain_batch), and each run holds the advisory lock through NO-TIMEOUT LLM enrich calls (observed 437s + 131s holds, earlier comment) — minutes per <=5 entries against enqueues from every attest/cert (plan_review/attest.py:218) plus the drain's own stale-digest self-heal re-enqueues (enrich_drain.py:293). maybe_drain probes on BOTH write seams (event_append.py, push.py), so a multi-event command pays the ~3s tax repeatedly.\n\nRELATED DATUM: another session observed tracker writes stalling minutes-long attributable to the enrich drain and worked around it with REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN=off. Post-fix I did not reproduce a minutes-long stall from the gate alone (53.5s worst command, dominated by push retries), but per-write multi-second taxes compound across a command's appends and across 20 concurrent agents; the off-switch workaround remains effective and safe (drain is deferrable by design).\n\nWHY NOT A SMALL MECHANICAL FIX (verdict rationale):\n- Budget recalibration or warning throttling (AC option) silences the observability without removing the multi-second per-write tax or the backlog growth — it would mask, not fix.\n- An append-tolerant marker (per-ticket dirty set) fixes only the marker-churn arm; the scoped walk stays O(backlog) and the backlog stays ~1,050.\n- The real fix must change the drain's throughput/placement contract — some combination of: (1) an existence-only gate probe (maybe_drain needs bool, not the sorted list — early-exit on first pending hit is O(1) when backlogged and marker-fast when quiet); (2) LLM calls moved OUTSIDE the advisory lock + a batch cap sized to the enqueue rate, or the already-mooted dedicated-clone drain (see 9c72 relates_to); (3) backlog policy for the standing ~1,050 (age-out/tombstone or bulk-drain). (1) is the cheapest and nearly mechanical but still changes the gate contract this ticket says needs a recorded decision, and alone it does not drain the backlog or shorten lock holds — the write-stall datum stays live.\n\nDISPOSITION: not implementing under this assessment (operator instruction: architectural changes escalate). Options above are the decision menu for AC1. Ticket returned to open for the operator."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787442566224477001,"uuid":"400e48e2-f633-4ec1-9b41-27bf811b2de8"}